ERR1 = "Provide an explicit equality operator for a type with an access or floating-point component"
ERR2 = "Define /= to match an equality operator that returns a non-Boolean result"


def ids():
    return ("ADA95_5.7.5",)


def name(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_5.7.5": "Published Standards/Ada 95/5.7.5 Overloading the Equality Operator",
    }[id]


def tags(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_5.7.5": [
            "Language: Ada",
            "Standard: Ada 95",
            "Operators",
        ],
    }.get(id, [])


def detailed_description(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_5.7.5": """
<p><b>Guideline</b></p>
<p>&#8226; Define an appropriate equality operator for private types.</p>
<p>&#8226; Consider redefining the equality operator for a private type.</p>
<p>&#8226; When overloading the equality operator for types, maintain the properties of an algebraic equivalence relation.</p>

<p><b>Example</b></p>
<pre><code language="Ada">type Measurement is record
   Value : Float;
end record;

function "=" (Left, Right : Measurement) return Boolean is
   Epsilon : constant Float := Float'Model_Epsilon * 1000.0;
begin
   return abs (Left.Value - Right.Value) <=
      Epsilon * Float'Max (abs (Left.Value), abs (Right.Value));
end "=";</code></pre>

<p><b>Rationale</b></p>
<p>The predefined equality operation provided with private types depends on the data structure chosen to implement that type.
If access types are used, then equality will mean the operands have the same pointer value.
If discrete types are used, then equality will mean the operands have the same value.
If a floating-point type is used, then equality is based on Ada model intervals (see Guideline 7.2.7).
You should, therefore, redefine equality to provide the meaning expected by the client.
If you implement a private type using an access type, you should redefine equality to provide a deep equality.
For floating-point types, you may want to provide an equality that tests for equality within some application-dependent epsilon value.</p>
<p>Any assumptions about the meaning of equality for private types will create a dependency on the implementation of that type.
See Gonzalez (1991) for a detailed discussion.</p>
<p>When the definition of "=" is provided, there is a conventional algebraic meaning implied by this symbol.
As described in Baker (1991), the following properties should remain true for the equality operator:</p>
<p>Reflexive: a = a</p>
<p>Symmetric: a = b ==> b = a</p>
<p>Transitive: a = b and b = c ==> a = c</p>
<p>In redefining equality, you are not required to have a result type of Standard.Boolean.
The Rationale (1995, §6.3) gives two examples where your result type is a user-defined type.
In a three-valued logic abstraction, you redefine equality to return one of True, False, or Unknown.
In a vector processing application, you can define a component-wise equality operator that returns a vector of Boolean values.
In both these instances, you should also redefine inequality because it is not the Boolean complement of the equality function.</p>

<p><b>Developer's Note</b></p>
<p>Two structural cases are automated.
First, a record, array, or private type with an access-type or floating-point component (or, for an array, element type) that has no user-defined same-type <code>"="</code> &#8212; one whose two parameters are both the type itself, since a mixed-type overload does not address same-type comparisons &#8212; is flagged as a candidate for review, since the predefined equality is exactly the case this guideline calls out as unreliable for those component kinds.
Second, an equality operator that returns something other than <code>Boolean</code> is flagged when no matching <code>"/="</code> is defined for the same type, since Ada will not derive one automatically.
The equivalence-relation requirement (reflexive, symmetric, transitive) is not automated, since it depends on what the operator's implementation actually does, not its structure.
A derived (tagged) type that adds no components of its own is only checked for its own declared components, not components inherited from an ancestor, so a risky inherited component with no equality defined anywhere in the hierarchy may not be flagged.
When reviewing a flagged type, confirm any <code>"="</code> declared nearby is actually for that type &#8212; a <code>"="</code> for a different, unrelated type in the same file does not resolve the violation.</p>
""",
    }[id]


def test_entity(file):
    return file.kind().check("Ada File")


PREDEFINED_FLOAT_NAMES = {"FLOAT", "LONG_FLOAT",
                          "SHORT_FLOAT", "LONG_LONG_FLOAT"}


def declaration_tokens(type_ent):
    def_ref = type_ent.ref("Declare")

    if not def_ref:
        return []

    lex = def_ref.file().lexer().lexeme(def_ref.line(), def_ref.column())
    tokens = []

    while lex and lex.text() != ";":
        tokens.append(lex.text())
        lex = lex.next(True, True)

    return tokens


def is_float_type(type_ent):
    # The predefined Float (and its variants) reports the same generic
    # "Ada Type" kind as every other type, with nothing to distinguish it
    # as numeric/floating-point -- unlike access types, which do get a
    # distinct kind. Matching by name covers the predefined case; for a
    # user-defined type, its own declaration is checked for the "digits"
    # keyword (a new floating-point type) or "is new <a known float>"
    # (one level of derivation from a known float type).
    if type_ent.name().upper() in PREDEFINED_FLOAT_NAMES:
        return True

    tokens = declaration_tokens(type_ent)

    if "digits" in tokens:
        return True

    if "new" in tokens:
        idx = tokens.index("new")

        if idx + 1 < len(tokens) and tokens[idx + 1].upper() in PREDEFINED_FLOAT_NAMES:
            return True

    return False


def has_risky_component(t):
    for comp in t.ents("Declare", "Component"):
        typed = comp.ref("Typed")

        if not typed:
            continue

        te = typed.ent()

        if te.kind().check("Type Access") or is_float_type(te):
            return True

    return False


def has_custom_equality(t):
    # An operation "for" t is not necessarily a same-type equality: a
    # mixed-type "=" (e.g. T vs. Integer) is still associated with T but
    # does not address T-vs-T comparisons, which is what this guideline is
    # actually about. Both parameters must resolve to t itself.
    for r in t.refs("Operation"):
        fn = r.ent()

        if fn.longname().rsplit(".", 1)[-1] != "=":
            continue

        params = fn.ents("Declare", "Parameter")

        if len(params) != 2:
            continue

        if all((p.ref("Typed") and p.ref("Typed").ent().id() == t.id()) for p in params):
            return True

    return False


def check_missing_equality(check, file):
    # A private type's partial declaration and its full-view completion
    # (often in the private part, or a separate body) are the same entity
    # with two separate "Declare"-kind refs -- without deduplicating, both
    # would be reported as independent violations for the one type.
    reported = set()

    for ref in file.filerefs("Declare", "Type Record"):
        t = ref.ent()

        if t.id() in reported:
            continue

        if has_custom_equality(t):
            continue

        if has_risky_component(t):
            check.violation(t, ref.file(), ref.line(), ref.column(), ERR1)
            reported.add(t.id())

    for ref in file.filerefs("Declare", "Type Array"):
        t = ref.ent()

        if t.id() in reported:
            continue

        if has_custom_equality(t):
            continue

        typed = t.ref("Typed")

        if not typed:
            continue

        te = typed.ent()

        if te.kind().check("Type Access") or is_float_type(te):
            check.violation(t, ref.file(), ref.line(), ref.column(), ERR1)
            reported.add(t.id())


def check_missing_inequality(check, file):
    all_operators = list(file.filerefs(
        "Declare Spec, Declare Body", "Function Operator"))

    def op_name(fn):
        return fn.longname().rsplit(".", 1)[-1]

    def param_type(fn):
        params = fn.ents("Declare", "Parameter")
        return params[0].type() if len(params) == 2 else None

    for ref in all_operators:
        fn = ref.ent()

        if op_name(fn) != "=":
            continue

        # A subprogram normally has both a spec and a body declaration, in
        # separate files, each producing its own ref here -- report only
        # from the spec, to avoid flagging the same entity twice.
        if ref.kind().check("Declare Body") and fn.refs("Declare Spec"):
            continue

        if fn.type() == "Boolean":
            continue

        ptype = param_type(fn)

        if ptype is None:
            continue

        has_matching_neq = any(
            op_name(other.ent()) == "/=" and param_type(other.ent()) == ptype
            for other in all_operators
        )

        if not has_matching_neq:
            check.violation(fn, ref.file(), ref.line(), ref.column(), ERR2)


def check(check, file):
    check_missing_equality(check, file)
    check_missing_inequality(check, file)
